"""Original, specific theory questions and data-based geographical exercises."""
from .common import item, num as N, rng

HISTORY_TEXT='''Parliament|3|A Money Bill has been passed by the Lok Sabha. State three limitations on the Rajya Sabha’s role in dealing with it.|The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend it as an equal chamber; it can recommend changes; it must return it within 14 days, after which the constitutional Money Bill procedure allows passage without its return. The Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations.
Parliament|3|Give three reasons why the Lok Sabha exercises stronger control over the Union Council of Ministers than the Rajya Sabha.|The Council is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha; a no-confidence motion there can require resignation; the Lok Sabha controls grants and the Money Bill process.
Parliament|3|State three features explaining why the Rajya Sabha is called a permanent house.|It is not dissolved as a whole; members have staggered six-year terms; approximately one-third retire every two years.
Parliament|4|Explain two exclusive powers of the Rajya Sabha and distinguish them from two powers shared by both houses.|It may authorise Parliament to legislate on a State List matter in the national interest and authorise creation of an All India Service by the required special resolution. Both houses participate in constitutional amendment and impeachment of the President; those are not exclusive Rajya Sabha powers.
Parliament|4|Distinguish the roles of the Speaker and the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha using four points.|The Speaker is elected by Lok Sabha members; the Vice-President is ex officio Rajya Sabha Chairman. They preside over their respective houses and enforce procedure. The Speaker certifies a Money Bill. A joint sitting is normally presided over by the Lok Sabha Speaker, not by the Rajya Sabha Chairman.
Parliament|3|A member wants to ask the government for an explanation of an urgent matter. Explain Question Hour, an adjournment motion and a no-confidence motion as different accountability devices.|Question Hour permits questions to ministers; an adjournment motion interrupts normal business for a definite urgent public matter under the rules; a no-confidence motion tests the Lok Sabha’s confidence in the Council of Ministers.
Parliament|4|Explain four legislative functions of Parliament, keeping law-making distinct from financial control.|It makes laws on Union subjects; legislates on Concurrent subjects; can amend the Constitution under its prescribed procedure; and can legislate on State subjects in constitutionally specified situations, such as an authorised national-interest resolution or emergency provisions.
Parliament|3|Distinguish an ordinary bill from a Money Bill with reference to introduction, Rajya Sabha powers and a joint sitting.|An ordinary bill may generally originate in either house; a Money Bill originates only in Lok Sabha on the required recommendation. Both houses have substantial ordinary-bill powers, while Rajya Sabha only recommends on a Money Bill. Joint sitting provisions address specified ordinary-bill deadlocks, not Money Bills.
Executive|3|Explain how the President is the constitutional head while the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers exercise effective executive authority.|Executive acts are formally taken in the President’s name; the President ordinarily acts on the aid and advice of the Council; the Prime Minister heads the Council and directs the government enjoying Lok Sabha confidence.
Executive|3|State three legislative powers of the President.|Summoning/proroguing Parliament; addressing Parliament as prescribed and sending messages; assent to bills and promulgation of ordinances when constitutional conditions are met. Dissolution of Lok Sabha is another accepted relevant point.
Executive|4|Explain four steps/features of the constitutional impeachment process for the President.|A charge of violation of the Constitution may originate in either house; the prescribed notice/signature and special-majority requirements apply; the other house investigates or causes investigation with a right of defence; a resolution supported by at least two-thirds of that house’s total membership removes the President. The initiating resolution also requires the constitutional two-thirds-total-membership majority.
Executive|3|Give three financial powers or functions associated with the President.|Prior recommendation for introduction of a Money Bill; the annual financial statement is laid before Parliament in the President’s name; appointment of the Finance Commission. Authorisation of advances from the Contingency Fund is also relevant.
Executive|3|Explain collective responsibility of ministers to the Lok Sabha using a concrete consequence for a lost confidence vote.|Ministers publicly support Cabinet decisions as one team; the Council remains in office while it enjoys Lok Sabha confidence; loss of confidence requires the Council as a whole to resign, not merely one minister.
Executive|4|Give four ways in which the Prime Minister leads the Union government.|Selects/recommends ministers; allocates or coordinates portfolios and policy; chairs Cabinet meetings; acts as the main link between President and Council and communicates decisions. Leading the parliamentary majority is an additional relevant point.
Executive|3|Distinguish the Cabinet from the wider Council of Ministers in composition, frequency of meetings and policy-making.|Cabinet is the smaller senior group within the Council; it meets regularly as the main decision-making body; the Council includes other ranks and does not ordinarily meet collectively for every policy decision.
Executive|4|Classify these presidential functions and explain each classification: appointing ministers, assenting to a bill, granting a pardon, and appointing the Finance Commission.|Appointment: executive; assent: legislative; pardon: judicial/clemency; Finance Commission: financial. Give one explanatory clause linking each action to its category rather than four unexplained labels.
Judiciary|3|State three constitutional qualifications for appointment as a Supreme Court judge.|Indian citizenship plus the prescribed judicial/advocacy qualification: at least five years as a High Court judge, at least ten years as a High Court advocate, or a distinguished jurist in the President’s opinion. Do not treat all alternative professional qualifications as simultaneously compulsory.
Judiciary|4|Explain four safeguards of judicial independence.|Secure tenure with removal only by the prescribed process; protected service conditions/salaries as constitutionally provided; separation from routine executive direction in adjudication; restrictions on discussion of judicial conduct except appropriate removal proceedings. Post-retirement practice restrictions for Supreme Court judges are another relevant safeguard.
Judiciary|3|Distinguish original, appellate and advisory jurisdiction of the Supreme Court.|Original: specified cases begin there, including certain federal disputes and rights remedies; appellate: hears eligible appeals from lower courts; advisory: the President may seek an opinion under Article 143, which is advisory rather than an ordinary executable judgment.
Judiciary|4|Explain judicial review and name three kinds of government action that may raise a constitutional challenge.|Judicial review examines conformity with the Constitution. A law allegedly violating fundamental rights, legislation outside the enacting body’s competence, or unconstitutional executive action may be reviewed. Courts do not replace policy merely because they prefer another policy.
Judiciary|3|A person is unlawfully detained. Name the relevant writ and explain its purpose. Contrast it with mandamus.|Habeas corpus requires production of the detained person and examination of the legality of detention; it protects personal liberty. Mandamus directs a public authority to perform a public/legal duty it has failed to perform.
Judiciary|4|Give four advantages of Lok Adalats in suitable disputes.|They promote settlement by conciliation; reduce delay; reduce expense; ease the ordinary courts’ burden and improve access. Their role should not be described as a universal replacement for trials in every kind of case.
Judiciary|3|State who appoints High Court judges, the retirement age, and one relevant professional qualification.|The President appoints them through the constitutional process; retirement age is 62; at least ten years’ judicial office in India or at least ten years as an advocate of a High Court is a relevant alternative, along with citizenship.
Judiciary|4|Distinguish the Supreme Court and High Courts with four carefully matched points.|Supreme Court is the national apex; High Courts head state/territorial judicial systems. Retirement ages are 65 and 62. Supreme Court rights writ power is under Article 32, High Courts under Article 226 with a wider purpose scope. Their territorial and appellate positions differ within the integrated system.
1857|3|Explain three ways in which British economic policies contributed to the Revolt of 1857.|Heavy revenue demands burdened cultivators; decline of crafts/competition from British manufactures hurt artisans; annexations and loss of patronage affected dependants of Indian courts. Link each policy to the affected group.
1857|3|Explain the Enfield cartridge issue as the immediate cause of the revolt, distinguishing it from long-term grievances.|Cartridges were believed to be greased with cow/pig fat; biting them offended Hindu/Muslim religious beliefs; this triggered existing resentment rather than creating all discontent by itself.
1857|3|What was the General Service Enlistment Act, and why did some sepoys resent it? Give the policy and two related concerns.|The 1856 measure required new recruits to accept overseas service when ordered. Crossing seas conflicted with some religious/caste beliefs; it increased anxiety about British interference and service conditions. Explain resentment without claiming every soldier shared one belief.
1857|4|Explain four changes in British policy or administration after the Revolt of 1857.|Company rule ended and authority passed to the Crown; policy reassured princes and renounced indiscriminate annexation; assurances were given about religious non-interference; the army was reorganised with a higher European proportion and changed recruitment/command arrangements.
1857|4|Explain four reasons for the revolt’s failure.|Limited territorial spread; lack of unified leadership and common political programme; superior British organisation/resources/communications; lack of sustained support from several princes and groups. Support each reason with its practical effect.
1857|3|Explain three socio-religious causes of resentment before 1857.|Fear of conversion linked with missionary activity; suspicion of interference with customs and inheritance laws; racial discrimination and anxiety about unfamiliar reforms. Show how fear and distrust connected these developments.
1857|4|Give four army changes introduced after the revolt and explain their purpose.|Higher European troop proportion; artillery largely retained under European control; recruitment reorganised to prevent broad unity; different communities/regions balanced within forces. These changes aimed to reduce the risk of another united military rebellion.
1857|3|Compare political grievances of rulers with military grievances of sepoys in the revolt.|Rulers feared annexation/loss of authority and honours; sepoys resented unequal pay/promotion and service changes; the cartridge issue connected military grievance with religious fear. Different groups joined for overlapping, not identical, motives.
Nationalism|3|Explain three contributions of the press to the growth of Indian nationalism.|It criticised exploitative policies; spread political ideas and information across regions; linked educated opinion and exposed common grievances, supporting a sense of shared national interest.
Nationalism|4|Compare Early and Assertive Nationalists using aims, methods, attitude to British rule and mass participation.|Early leaders pursued constitutional reform through petitions, resolutions and persuasion, often trusting liberal British opinion; Assertive leaders stressed self-reliance and Swaraj, boycott/Swadeshi/national education and broader mobilisation. Avoid describing all Early leaders as loyal to every colonial policy.
Nationalism|3|State three early objectives of the Indian National Congress.|Promote contacts and unity among political workers; develop national feeling beyond regional/religious divisions; formulate and present common demands and train public opinion.
Nationalism|4|Explain four ways the Partition of Bengal stimulated the national movement.|It was opposed as division of a politically active region; boycott of foreign goods expanded; Swadeshi industries/education received support; new public participation and assertive methods developed. Explain events and responses rather than merely listing dates.
Nationalism|3|Explain the official reason and nationalist interpretation of the 1905 Partition of Bengal, and identify the Viceroy associated with it.|Officially administrative convenience; nationalists saw a divide-and-rule attempt to weaken unity, including religious division; Lord Curzon was the Viceroy.
Nationalism|4|Explain four factors contributing to the formation of the Muslim League.|British encouragement of separate political identities; elite concerns about representation; the Simla Deputation/separate-electorate demand; perceived need to protect Muslim political interests in the contemporary setting. Avoid presenting the community as having one uniform view.
Nationalism|3|Explain the significance of Swadeshi, boycott and national education as related methods.|Swadeshi promoted indigenous goods; boycott withdrew support from imported goods/institutions; national education developed self-reliant institutions and national consciousness. Link all three to constructive and oppositional work.
Nationalism|4|Give four contributions of an Assertive Nationalist leader, naming the leader first.|For Tilak: popular festivals for political mobilisation; newspapers Kesari/Mahratta; insistence on Swaraj; support for Swadeshi/boycott/national education and national awareness. Accept another syllabus leader with four accurate matched contributions.
Gandhian movements|3|Give three causes of the Non-Cooperation Movement.|Rowlatt Act repression; Jallianwala Bagh and the Punjab wrongs; the Khilafat grievance and disillusionment with post-war policy. Explain the connection to withdrawal of cooperation.
Gandhian movements|4|Explain four features of the Non-Cooperation programme.|Surrender of titles; boycott of government educational institutions/courts/elections as proposed; boycott of foreign cloth; constructive promotion of khadi, national education and Hindu-Muslim unity. Specify that this was intended as non-violent withdrawal of cooperation.
Gandhian movements|3|Why was Non-Cooperation withdrawn after Chauri Chaura? State the incident’s significance and Gandhi’s reasoning.|Protesters killed policemen after setting a police station on fire; violence contradicted the movement’s non-violent discipline; Gandhi suspended the movement because preparation/self-restraint were inadequate for the planned struggle.
Gandhian movements|4|State four clauses of the Gandhi-Irwin Pact, including at least one Congress undertaking.|Government undertook release of political prisoners not convicted of violence, withdrawal of ordinances/prosecutions, restoration of eligible confiscated property and permission for peaceful salt collection/picketing. Congress agreed to suspend Civil Disobedience and attend the Second Round Table Conference. Any four accurate clauses, with both sides represented.
Gandhian movements|3|Explain why the Salt March was an effective beginning for Civil Disobedience.|Salt affected ordinary people across classes; breaking the law provided a clear non-violent act against colonial authority; the march mobilised public attention and widened participation.
Gandhian movements|4|Give four causes or immediate circumstances leading to Quit India in 1942.|Failure of the Cripps Mission; wartime hardships and discontent; threat of Japanese advance; demand for an immediate end to British rule rather than an uncertain post-war promise. Explain their combined effect.
Gandhian movements|3|Compare Non-Cooperation and Civil Disobedience in their central method and one major starting event each.|Non-Cooperation withdrew support from institutions and began amid Rowlatt/Punjab/Khilafat grievances; Civil Disobedience deliberately broke selected unjust laws, notably salt law through the Dandi March in 1930. Both were intended to be non-violent.
Gandhian movements|4|Explain four effects of the Gandhian mass movements on Indian politics.|Broadened popular participation; involved women and new social groups; made non-violent action/constructive work central political methods; challenged colonial legitimacy and strengthened the demand for self-rule. Distinguish political effects from claiming immediate independence after each movement.
Independence and Partition|3|Give three reasons Congress accepted partition under the 1947 plan.|Experience of obstruction/conflict in the interim government; fear of civil war and further bloodshed; belief that a smaller strong centre was preferable to an unworkable weak united arrangement; desire to avoid prolonged British rule. Any three explained reasons.
Independence and Partition|4|State four provisions of the Indian Independence Act of 1947.|Creation of two independent dominions from 15 August 1947; end of British sovereignty/control; constituent assemblies gained legislative authority pending constitutions; British paramountcy over princely states lapsed. Relevant provisions must not be confused with unrelated later republican arrangements.
Independence and Partition|3|Name the last Viceroy and explain two terms of his June 1947 plan.|Lord Mountbatten; partition arrangements for British India with relevant provincial decisions; transfer of power to successor dominions and lapse of paramountcy/princely-state arrangements. State two specific accurate terms.
Independence and Partition|4|Explain four contributions of Subhas Chandra Bose and the INA to the freedom struggle.|Leadership and reorganisation of INA; formation of the Provisional Government of Azad Hind; mobilisation of Indians overseas and the military campaign; political impact of INA trials and patriotic response. Do not claim that INA alone secured independence militarily.
Independence and Partition|3|Explain why the INA trials had political significance beyond the legal charges.|They generated widespread nationalist sympathy; united different sections in support; intensified disaffection and challenged colonial legitimacy despite the INA’s military defeat.
Independence and Partition|4|Distinguish the Cabinet Mission’s aim of preserving unity from the final 1947 partition arrangement using four points.|Cabinet Mission proposed a union with limited central subjects and provincial grouping; it sought to avoid division into sovereign states. The 1947 plan accepted partition and two successor dominions; transfer of power followed with different constitutional arrangements. Explain differences rather than mixing clauses from the two plans.
Wars and dictatorships|3|Explain the Sarajevo crisis as the immediate cause of the First World War.|Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand at Sarajevo; Austro-Hungarian action/ultimatum against Serbia; alliance commitments and mobilisation widened the crisis into war. Distinguish trigger from underlying tensions.
Wars and dictatorships|4|Explain four long-term causes of the First World War.|Aggressive nationalism; imperial/colonial rivalry; militarism and arms race; rival alliance systems. Link each to growing distrust and the spread of conflict.
Wars and dictatorships|3|State three ways the Treaty of Versailles weakened Germany.|Territorial losses; military restrictions; reparations and war-guilt provisions. Explain the burden or humiliation of each rather than writing “harsh treaty” three times.
Wars and dictatorships|4|Explain four factors helping the rise of dictatorships in inter-war Europe.|Economic distress/unemployment; resentment over peace settlements or disappointed national aims; weak parliamentary governments; fear of communism and propaganda/paramilitary methods. Relate factors accurately to Germany/Italy as appropriate.
Wars and dictatorships|3|State three common ideological features of Fascism and Nazism.|Dictatorial one-party leadership; aggressive nationalism and militarism; suppression of opposition and individual freedoms. Nazism’s racial ideology must not be treated as identical in every detail to Italian Fascism.
Wars and dictatorships|4|Explain four causes of the Second World War.|Aggressive expansion by dictatorships; weaknesses/failure of collective security and the League; appeasement; grievances from Versailles and the immediate invasion of Poland. Distinguish long-term conditions from the immediate trigger.
Wars and dictatorships|3|Explain the policy of appeasement and two reasons it encouraged aggression.|Concessions were made to aggressive powers in hope of preserving peace; failure to enforce limits encouraged further demands; it weakened collective resistance and gave aggressors time/confidence to expand.
Wars and dictatorships|4|Explain four consequences of the Second World War.|Defeat of Axis powers; huge human/material loss; formation of the United Nations; emergence of US/USSR rivalry and the Cold War, with decolonisation accelerated in the wider aftermath. Use four distinct consequences.
Wars and dictatorships|3|Compare the League of Nations and the United Nations in origin and purpose.|League followed WWI; UN followed WWII; both aimed at peace/cooperation but differed in structure and enforcement arrangements. Their existence did not automatically guarantee collective action.
UN and NAM|3|State three purposes of the United Nations.|Maintain international peace/security; develop friendly relations among nations; cooperate on economic/social/humanitarian problems and human rights. Explain each distinct purpose.
UN and NAM|4|Describe the composition and voting of the Security Council using four points.|Fifteen members; five permanent members; ten elected non-permanent members with two-year terms; substantive decisions require the prescribed majority without a negative vote/veto by a permanent member. Do not say every member has veto power.
UN and NAM|3|State three functions of the General Assembly.|Discuss issues within the Charter and make recommendations; approve the budget/apportion expenses; elect or participate in elections of members of UN organs under the Charter. It does not possess the same binding enforcement powers as the Security Council.
UN and NAM|4|Explain four distinctions between the General Assembly and Security Council.|Membership: all UN members versus fifteen; representation/voting: one state one vote versus permanent/non-permanent categories; veto: absent in Assembly but present for permanent Council members on substantive decisions; role: deliberation/recommendations versus primary responsibility for peace/security under the Charter.
UN and NAM|3|State the composition and principal judicial role of the International Court of Justice.|Fifteen judges; elected for nine-year terms through General Assembly and Security Council; settles submitted legal disputes between states and gives advisory opinions when properly requested. It is not a criminal trial court for ordinary individual offences.
UN and NAM|4|Give one principal field of work and one concrete type of contribution each for WHO and UNESCO.|WHO: international public health, such as disease-control coordination/health standards; UNESCO: education, science and culture, such as heritage protection/literacy and educational cooperation. Two accurate field-example pairs earn four points.
UN and NAM|3|Name three architects of the Non-Aligned Movement and explain its basic position.|Nehru, Tito and Nasser are accepted architects (others include Sukarno and Nkrumah). NAM sought independent judgement rather than membership of Cold War power blocs; it did not require indifference to world affairs.
UN and NAM|4|Explain four objectives/principles associated with NAM.|Independent foreign policy; opposition to colonialism/racism; peaceful coexistence and settlement of disputes; disarmament and reduced bloc confrontation. Explain how these differ from passive neutrality in every conflict.
UN and NAM|3|A student says “non-alignment means never taking a position.” Correct the statement with three points.|It rejects automatic alignment with a power bloc; members judge issues on their merits; active support for peace, decolonisation and cooperation is consistent with non-alignment.
UN and NAM|4|Explain why the UN and NAM are not interchangeable organisations using four distinctions.|UN is a universal Charter-based international organisation; NAM is a grouping centred on independent non-bloc policy. Their membership principles differ; UN has formal principal organs such as the Council/ICJ; NAM does not replace those Charter mechanisms or hold the Council’s enforcement powers.'''

GEO_TEXT='''Climate|3|Explain why Chennai receives a substantial part of its rainfall in the retreating-monsoon season while Mumbai receives most rain during the southwest monsoon.|Chennai’s east-coast position exposes it to northeast/retreating winds gaining moisture over the Bay of Bengal; Mumbai faces moisture-bearing southwest winds from the Arabian Sea; relief/seasonal wind direction affects the contrasting distribution.
Climate|3|Explain why a coastal city usually has a smaller annual temperature range than an inland city at a similar latitude.|Water heats/cools more slowly than land; onshore influence moderates seasonal extremes; inland locations lack the same maritime moderation. Latitude alone does not account for the contrast.
Climate|3|Explain three ways the Himalayas influence India’s climate.|They obstruct cold continental winds; act as a barrier helping monsoon uplift/rainfall; contribute to regional distribution and the protection/modification of winter conditions. Explain each link rather than only naming the range.
Climate|3|Why can northwestern India receive winter rain? State the source, a region affected and one agricultural benefit.|Western disturbances bring winter precipitation to northwestern India, including Punjab/Haryana; it can benefit the rabi wheat crop. Do not attribute this rainfall to the main southwest monsoon.
Climate|4|Compare southwest and northeast monsoon winds using direction, season, moisture source and one rainfall region.|Southwest winds dominate the summer rainy season, carrying moisture from the Arabian Sea/Bay; northeast winds are the retreating/cool-season flow, generally offshore but can gain Bay moisture and rain on the southeast coast. Match each characteristic to the correct season/region.
Soils|3|Explain three properties that make black soil suitable for cotton cultivation, and give one management difficulty.|It retains moisture; is deep/clayey; contains useful mineral nutrients and develops cracks aiding aeration when dry. Wet stickiness and drainage/working difficulties need management. Credit three relevant property-function links, not “black colour” alone.
Soils|3|Distinguish khadar and bhangar by age, location and fertility characteristics.|Khadar is newer alluvium on active floodplains, replenished by floods; bhangar is older alluvium on higher terraces, often with kankar/calcareous nodules. Younger replenished deposits are generally more fertile.
Soils|4|Explain formation and two limitations of laterite soil, then give one way to make cultivation more productive.|High temperature/heavy seasonal rain promote leaching; many bases/nutrients are removed and humus can be low; fertility is limited without management. Manuring/fertilisation and suitable conservation/crop choices improve productivity. Four linked points.
Soils|3|A farm has red soil with low humus and limited water-holding capacity. Explain the colour and recommend two improvement measures.|Iron compounds give the red colour; organic manure improves humus/moisture retention; appropriate irrigation and balanced fertilisers address water/nutrient limitations.
Soils|4|Distinguish sheet and gully erosion and recommend a suitable control for each.|Sheet erosion removes a broad thin layer; gully erosion cuts deep channels. Vegetative cover/contour cultivation reduce sheet runoff; check dams, bunds and vegetation help stabilise gullies. Match each measure to the process.
Natural vegetation|3|Why are tropical evergreen forests difficult to exploit commercially despite their timber resources?|Dense mixed-species growth makes single-species extraction difficult; tangled undergrowth and wet conditions hinder access; scattered valuable trees and transport difficulties raise effort/cost.
Natural vegetation|3|Explain why tropical deciduous forests shed leaves during a dry season and why they are often easier to exploit than evergreen forests.|Seasonal leaf shedding conserves water; relatively open stands and commercially useful species assist access; drier working seasons ease extraction compared with dense wet mixed forests.
Natural vegetation|4|A coastal delta has saline tidal water, muddy ground and poorly aerated soil. Identify the vegetation type and explain three adaptations/associations.|Mangrove/littoral vegetation; salt tolerance; specialised support/aerating roots help in unstable/oxygen-poor mud; growth is associated with tidal coastal environments. Do not call every wet inland forest a mangrove.
Natural vegetation|3|A region receives less than about 70 cm annual rainfall. Describe three likely adaptations of natural vegetation.|Thorn/scrub vegetation often has small leaves/spines, deep roots, thick bark/waxy surfaces and sparse spacing to conserve/access water. Explain three structure-function links.
Natural vegetation|4|Explain four benefits of forest conservation beyond timber supply.|Limits soil erosion; helps regulate water/catchments; supports biodiversity/habitats; stores carbon and supports local livelihoods. Explain four distinct functions, not four forest-product examples.
Water resources|3|Why may tank irrigation be suitable in parts of peninsular India? Give two physical reasons and one limitation.|Undulating/hard-rock terrain offers natural depressions and limits canal development in some places; seasonal storage can be used. Evaporation, silting and seasonal drying limit reliability.
Water resources|4|Compare canals and wells using source, terrain, reliability and one risk.|Canals divert stored/river water and work best with suitable gradients, supporting large areas but risking waterlogging/salinity; wells draw groundwater with local control but depend on aquifer recharge and pumping conditions. Match comparisons to the stated criterion.
Water resources|3|A farmer has limited water and widely spaced orchard trees. Recommend irrigation and give two reasons.|Drip irrigation: water is delivered near root zones; evaporation/runoff losses and unnecessary wetting between trees are reduced. Explain suitability for the spaced crop rather than naming a method alone.
Water resources|3|A farm has uneven sandy land and crops suitable for overhead watering. Recommend an irrigation method and give two advantages under those conditions.|Sprinkler irrigation can distribute water without extensive land levelling and suits permeable soil/uneven fields; control application to reduce wastage. Wind/evaporation limitations should be recognised if asked.
Water resources|4|Explain four steps in a rooftop rainwater-harvesting system and their purpose.|Collect roof runoff; convey through gutters/pipes; filter and divert the dirty first flush; store safely or recharge groundwater through a suitable structure. Link each part to collection/quality/storage/recharge.
Minerals and energy|3|Explain three reasons for conserving mineral resources.|They are exhaustible on human time scales; extraction causes environmental/social costs; conservation/recycling improves long-term availability and reduces waste. Do not claim minerals regrow like crops.
Minerals and energy|4|Compare coal-based electricity and solar electricity using source, environmental impact, reliability and a practical limitation.|Coal consumes an exhaustible fuel and produces combustion emissions but can generate on demand with fuel supply. Solar uses sunlight with low operational emissions but varies with light and requires area/storage/grid support. Avoid claiming either has no environmental cost.
Minerals and energy|3|Why are efficient transport links important for a petroleum refinery? Give three connected reasons.|Crude must reach the refinery; products must reach dispersed markets; bulk, continuous handling through ports/pipelines/rail/road reduces cost and interruptions. Explain links to raw material, distribution and scale.
Minerals and energy|4|Give two advantages and two limitations of hydroelectricity.|Renewable flowing-water input; no fuel combustion during generation. High initial works/possible displacement/ecological change and dependence on suitable relief/water supply are limitations. Give four distinct supported points.
Agriculture|3|Compare rice and wheat using growing season, temperature and moisture requirements.|Rice is predominantly a warm, wet kharif crop with high water needs; wheat is mainly rabi, favours cooler growing conditions and warmer dry conditions at ripening, with moderate moisture. State matched comparisons.
Agriculture|3|Explain why tea cultivation often uses well-drained slopes, plentiful rain and substantial labour.|Slopes drain excess water while retaining moist conditions; rain supports leaf growth; frequent careful plucking and processing require labour. Do not say tea roots benefit from stagnant water.
Agriculture|4|Explain four links between sugarcane characteristics and the location/operation of sugar mills.|Cane is bulky; transport is costly; sugar recovery falls after cutting, requiring quick crushing; seasonal harvest concentrates processing. Mills near producing areas reduce delay and handling losses.
Agriculture|3|Why does cotton benefit from a long frost-free period and dry weather during picking? Add one suitable soil property.|Warm frost-free conditions support growth; rain during picking can damage lint/quality; moisture-retentive black soil helps bridge dry spells while drainage matters.
Agriculture|4|Compare plantation farming and intensive subsistence farming using crop purpose, scale, inputs and labour.|Plantations usually specialise in commercial perennial crops on large estates with processing/market links; intensive subsistence uses small holdings and intensive family labour to produce food, often with multiple cropping. Compare each stated criterion, acknowledging variation.
Manufacturing|3|Explain three locational requirements of an iron and steel plant.|Access to bulky iron ore/coal/fluxes; dependable power and water; transport/market links. Link requirements to high-volume material handling and production.
Manufacturing|4|Compare an agro-based industry and a mineral-based industry using named examples, raw material, location and one constraint.|Example sugar or cotton textiles uses agricultural material; iron/steel uses mineral inputs. Agro industries may be tied to crop season/perishability; mineral industries to bulky input/power/transport. Give one named and matched comparison for each criterion.
Manufacturing|3|Why did humid conditions, a port and a cotton-growing hinterland favour the growth of a cotton-textile centre such as Mumbai?|Humidity reduced yarn breakage in traditional spinning; the port supported imports/exports; nearby cotton and transport supplied raw material/markets. Explain the historical association without treating climate as the only modern requirement.
Manufacturing|4|Explain four reasons industrial location can change over time.|Transport improvements reduce distance penalties; new energy/technology changes inputs; markets/labour/skills shift; policy, land and environmental constraints alter costs. Use a named type of industry to illustrate rather than asserting a fixed rule forever.
Transport|3|For fresh milk distribution to villages within 30 km without railway stations, recommend transport and justify it with three reasons.|Road transport: door-to-door access; flexible routes/timing for dispersed villages; limited transfer reduces delay/damage for a perishable product. Distance and access favour it over fixed rail terminals.
Transport|3|Give three advantages of railways for long-distance bulk freight over road transport.|Large loads per train; comparatively economical high-volume long hauls; lower rolling friction/efficient corridor movement. Do not claim railways automatically reach every delivery address.
Transport|4|Give two advantages and two limitations of inland waterways for cargo.|Can be economical for heavy/bulky loads with low friction; useful along navigable corridors. Limitations include seasonal depth/silting and limited routes/speed/terminal access. Four distinct points.
Transport|3|Why is air transport used for some high-value urgent cargo despite high cost?|It saves time; high value/sensitivity can justify expense; reaches distant centres quickly when surface journeys would cause delay. Bulk low-value goods usually do not justify the same cost.
Transport|4|Compare road and rail transport using route flexibility, initial infrastructure, load scale and final delivery.|Roads offer flexible routes and door access, often lower initial entry for short distribution; rail needs fixed tracks/terminals but handles large regular bulk loads efficiently; road vehicles often provide the final delivery link. Use matched points.
Waste management|3|A school has vegetable peels, clean paper and used batteries. Assign a safe management route to each and explain why mixing them is undesirable.|Peels: compost/biogas where appropriate; paper: reuse/recycle; batteries: authorised separate hazardous/e-waste handling. Mixing contaminates recoverable material and can introduce toxic substances into compost.
Waste management|4|Explain the refuse, reduce, reuse and recycle hierarchy through four different school examples.|Refuse avoidable single-use items; reduce unnecessary material consumption; reuse folders/containers; recycle clean segregated paper through suitable collection. Give an example for each and do not describe disposal as recycling.
Waste management|3|Explain three disadvantages of open dumping near a water body.|Leachate contaminates water; vectors and odour affect health/living conditions; waste can obstruct drainage and harm aquatic organisms. Explain mechanisms rather than repeating “pollution”.
Waste management|4|Distinguish composting and incineration using suitable waste, process, products and one limitation.|Composting biologically decomposes suitable organic waste into useful material but needs segregation/control; incineration burns appropriate waste, reduces volume but requires emission controls and ash management. Hazardous items are not automatically safe for either route.
Waste management|3|Why must infectious medical waste and electronic waste be segregated from ordinary household waste?|Medical waste can transmit infection or contain sharps; electronic waste may contain toxic recoverable components; both need authorised specialised treatment/recovery rather than uncontrolled mixing or burning.'''

def parse(sid,text,section):
    result=[]
    for line in text.splitlines():
        if not line.strip():continue
        topic,marks,prompt,answer=line.split('|',3)
        result.append(item(topic,prompt,answer,int(marks),id=f'{sid}-{len(result)+1:03}',section=section,family=topic))
    return result

def history_bank():
    bank=parse('histciv',HISTORY_TEXT,'Structured theory')
    # More demanding comparisons are distinct questions, not reworded duplicates.
    comparisons=[
      ('1857','Gandhian movements','Compare the role of armed rebellion in 1857 with the intended discipline of Gandhian mass movements. Use methods, leadership and participation.','1857 involved military/civil rebellion with varied regional leadership and motives; Gandhian movements sought disciplined non-violent mass mobilisation. Both challenged colonial rule, but methods, coordination and political setting differed.'),
      ('Wars and dictatorships','UN and NAM','Explain how experience of two world wars encouraged both international organisation and a desire to avoid power-bloc domination. Distinguish the UN from NAM.','War destruction encouraged collective peace/security through the UN; later Cold War rivalry led NAM members to independent non-bloc judgement. The organisations arose in related international conditions but have different structures and roles.'),
      ('Parliament','Executive','Explain why the formal executive head and the politically responsible government are different in India. Link the President, Prime Minister and Lok Sabha.','President is constitutional head; Council headed by PM ordinarily provides aid/advice and directs policy; Council’s collective responsibility to Lok Sabha links executive government to elected-house confidence.'),
      ('Judiciary','Executive','Compare presidential clemency and a court’s judicial review. Why are these not interchangeable?','Clemency affects punishment through a constitutional executive power; judicial review examines legal/constitutional validity. Both have defined limits but one is not an ordinary appellate substitute for the other.'),
    ]
    for a,b,prompt,answer in comparisons:bank.append(item(a,prompt,answer,4,id=f'histciv-{len(bank)+1:03}',section='Comparison and application',family=a+' / '+b))
    return bank

def geography_bank():
    bank=parse('geo',GEO_TEXT,'Theory by unit')
    def add(q,section):q.update(id=f'geo-{len(bank)+1:04}',section=section);bank.append(q)
    for v in range(80):
        r=rng('geodata',v); scale=r.choice([25000,50000,100000]); cm=r.randint(15,95)/10; east=r.randint(20,70); north=r.randint(10,80); tenthe=r.randint(1,8); tenthn=r.randint(1,8); ref=f'{east}{tenthe}{north}{tenthn}'
        add(item('Topographical interpretation',f'A map has scale 1:{scale}. A straight road measures {cm:.1f} cm on it. Find its actual length in kilometres. A point lies {tenthe} tenths east of easting {east} and {tenthn} tenths north of northing {north}, within that grid square. Give its six-figure reference.',f'Distance={cm}×{scale}/100000={N(cm*scale/100000)} km. Grid reference={ref}: read easting first, then northing.',4),'Text-only topography')
    for v in range(80):
        r=rng('climatedata',v); wet=r.choice(['summer','winter']); base=r.randint(12,22) if wet=='summer' else r.randint(22,25); peak=base+(r.randint(12,18) if wet=='summer' else r.randint(8,11)); temps=[base,base+2,base+5,peak-2,peak,peak-3,peak-5,peak-5,peak-4,base+6,base+3,base+1]; rainfall=[r.randint(0,25) for _ in range(12)]
        for m in ([5,6,7,8] if wet=='summer' else [9,10,11]):rainfall[m]+=r.randint(70,200)
        location='inland India' if wet=='summer' else 'the southeast coast of India'
        add(item('Climate',f'A hypothetical station in {location} has Jan–Dec temperatures (°C) {temps} and rainfall (mm) {rainfall}. Calculate annual temperature range and total rainfall. Identify the main wet season and infer whether summer or retreating/northeast monsoon influence is more likely, using both the rainfall pattern and the stated location.',f'Range={max(temps)}−{min(temps)}={max(temps)-min(temps)}°C; total rain={sum(rainfall)} mm. Main wet season: '+('June–September, consistent with southwest/summer monsoon influence.' if wet=='summer' else 'October–December, consistent with retreating/northeast monsoon influence on the stated southeast coast.')+' These are model data; identifying an exact real city is not required.',4),'Climate data interpretation')
    places=[('Western Ghats','a range along the western side of peninsular India, associated with orographic rain'),('Eastern Ghats','discontinuous hills along the eastern side of the peninsula'),('Himalayas','the northern mountain system influencing cold-air and monsoon conditions'),('Thar Desert','an arid region of northwestern India'),('Ganga','a major river of the northern plains flowing towards the Bay of Bengal'),('Narmada','a major west-flowing peninsular river'),('Tapi','a west-flowing river south of the Narmada'),('Godavari','a major east-flowing peninsular river'),('Krishna','an east-flowing peninsular river south of the Godavari'),('Kaveri','a southern peninsular river flowing to the Bay of Bengal'),('Mumbai','a major west-coast port in Maharashtra'),('Chennai','an east-coast port city in Tamil Nadu'),('Kolkata','a major city and port associated with the Hooghly'),('Delhi','the national capital on the northern plains'),('Deccan Plateau','the large peninsular plateau south of the northern plains')]
    for name,description in places:
        add(item('India location knowledge',f'Identify the prescribed Indian feature described as “{description}”. State whether it is a river, relief feature or city/port and give its broad region. No map drawing is required.',f'{name}. Description: {description}. Match its type and broad location to these identifying details.',2),'Text-only location knowledge')
    return bank
